    "body": "Novelis has produced the first aluminum coil on the cold mill at its $5 billion recycling and rolling plant in Bay Minette, Alabama. The company announced the milestone October 7 as it prepares to begin commercial shipments in the second quarter of 2027.\nWhat’s Related\nThe plant is still being commissioned, with equipment needed for commercial shipments going through that process. Novelis is also working toward customer qualification, keeping the first-coil milestone separate from the start of regular shipments to buyers.\nSteve Fisher, president and CEO of Novelis, described the milestone as “an important step in commissioning Bay Minette and increasing the supply of rolled aluminum in the United States.” He said the investment will help the company meet long-term demand in North America, where available aluminum production is constrained.\nFisher also said the project will increase the use of recycled material in Novelis products. The plant combines aluminum recycling and rolling at one location, bringing both operations together to supply customers in packaging , automotive manufacturing and other markets.\nShipments expected in 2027\nOnce fully operational, Bay Minette is expected to produce 600,000 metric tons of rolled aluminum annually. Its products will serve beverage packaging, automotive, and specialty-product customers, adding a large new source of U.S.-made aluminum for those buyers.\nThe April–June 2027 shipment target matches the company’s previously stated schedule, according to Aluminum Market Update. Novelis has already secured long-term customer agreements for the facility, according to its project website.\nRecycling more than 15 billion cans a year\nThe facility includes a recycling center designed to process more than 15 billion used beverage cans annually. That operation will supply recycled material for the plant’s aluminum products alongside its new rolling equipment.\nNovelis says Bay Minette is the first fully integrated aluminum recycling and rolling plant built in the United States in more than 40 years. The company expects the facility to create up to 1,000 new jobs once fully operational.",
    "body_sha256": "c487be645748531fda034d29ef1f95b37bd31eac6baa37387fc08d8189a22340",
